1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
At midnight tonight, gravity doubles.

2
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:13,000
No announcement. No flash in the sky.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:19,500
The only evidence is you — lying in your bed,

4
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:25,000
feeling like someone stacked a second person
on top of your chest.

5
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,500
You weigh a hundred and eighty pounds.

6
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:35,500
As of twelve-oh-one, your body presses
into the mattress with the force of
three hundred and sixty.

7
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:40,000
You haven't gained a single gram.

8
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:47,000
The pull of the planet beneath you simply
got twice as strong —

9
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:52,000
for you, for your house, for every rock
and bridge and bird on Earth.

10
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,000
Here's what the next forty-eight hours
actually look like.

11
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:05,000
And here's the part nobody expects: what happens
a year from now is stranger than anything
that happens tonight.

12
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,000
The first thing you notice isn't the weight.
It's the tiredness.

13
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:15,000
Getting out of bed, your arms — trained
for one gravity — now work at double load.

14
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:21,000
Standing up, your heart has to push blood
uphill against double the pull.

15
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:27,500
By mid-morning it pools in your legs,
your ankles swell, your breath comes
faster and shallower.

16
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:31,000
You're not heavier. Everything you do is.

17
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,000
Getting dressed is a workout.
The stairs are a mountain.

18
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,000
And you're one of the lucky ones.

19
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:44,000
Somewhere, a hospital patient who could
barely stand yesterday can't today.

20
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:50,000
An elderly parent who walked with a cane
no longer can.

21
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:56,000
For a small, quiet share of humanity,
double gravity is not an inconvenience.

22
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:58,800
It's the last straw.

23
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Your bedroom, meanwhile, is mostly fine.

24
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:08,000
Nothing has broken — furniture is built
with safety margins.

25
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:13,000
But the old shelf you never trusted
sags a little more.

26
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:17,500
The ceiling fan wobbles a little lower.

27
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:23,000
Outside, everyone moves the same way:
leaning forward, taking short steps,

28
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:27,000
like hikers carrying loaded packs.

29
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:31,000
Except every hiker is carrying themselves.

30
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,000
Birds are the first surprise.

31
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:39,000
A sparrow now weighs double — but here's
the twist almost everyone gets wrong.

32
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:45,000
When gravity doubles, the atmosphere
itself is pulled down and compressed.

33
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:50,500
The air at the surface becomes measurably
denser — in the simplest physics model,
nearly twice as dense.

34
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:55,000
Denser air means more lift for every wingbeat.

35
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:59,000
Twice the weight. Nearly twice the lift.

36
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:04,000
For a bird already on the wing,
the two cancel almost exactly.

37
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:10,000
What changes is everything around the flying:
takeoffs turn labored, landings arrive heavy,

38
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:14,000
and the survivors fly low and careful.

39
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:17,000
This is informed reasoning, not a certainty.

40
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:21,000
But the sky would look different by lunchtime.

41
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:27,000
The same near-cancellation happens for
the jetliners already airborne at midnight.

42
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:33,000
A cruising aircraft keeps flying — twice
the weight, twice the air, almost a wash.

43
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:40,000
But the drag on its body roughly doubles
too, so every mile burns close to
twice the fuel.

44
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:46,000
Flights start diverting within the hour,

45
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:53,000
and every landing now arrives with double
the weight on every wheel and double
the energy for the brakes to eat.

46
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:56,500
It's a bad night for runways.

47
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:01,000
Some of them are closed for good by morning.

48
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,000
Cars barely notice.

49
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,000
Your engine moves the car, not you.

50
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:10,000
Bridges are different.

51
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:17,000
Engineers design them with safety factors —
margin above expected loads.

52
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:22,000
Doubling live weight doesn't snap
the modern ones. It eats the margin.

53
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:29,000
The failures come one at a time, for weeks,
in the old bridges, the corroded ones,
the ones built cheap.

54
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:33,000
That's the pattern to remember:

55
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:36,000
nothing breaks at midnight.

56
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:41,000
Everything starts breaking in slow motion.

57
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:46,000
By evening, your spine has compressed
under the load. You're measurably shorter.

58
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:52,000
Your heart rate is up, your joints ache
in places you didn't know had places,

59
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:56,000
and standing feels like running used to.

60
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:03,000
There's real science here: fighter pilots
tolerate far more than two g —

61
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:08,000
for seconds, in pressure suits that squeeze
blood back toward their brains.

62
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:11,000
You're living at two g around
the clock, in pajamas.

63
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:19,000
Space agencies have literally spun volunteers
in centrifuges to study what sustained
hypergravity does to the body,

64
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:24,000
and even modest increases load bones,
hearts, and eyes measurably.

65
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:29,000
And here's the hopeful part — your body adapts.

66
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:32,000
Bones strengthen under load. That's what they're for.

67
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:37,000
Within weeks, you're functionally stronger
against the new normal.

68
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,000
The young and the healthy adapt.

69
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:45,000
The ones who can't, never leave the bed.

70
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:51,000
That's the first month's quiet arithmetic,
and nobody publishes the number.

71
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:57,000
Everything from this point forward
is speculation — labeled as such —

72
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:00,000
but it's speculation built on physics.

73
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:06,000
Raindrops hit harder — heavier drops
fall faster, and every storm lands
with more violence.

74
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:11,000
Waves carry double the weight
of water behind them.

75
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:17,000
Forests start shedding branches like
a bad haircut, and over years, the old
giants come down in windstorms, one at a time.

76
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:23,000
Architecture gets rebuilt lower, wider,
thicker — in a single generation,

77
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:27,000
the skyline becomes a skyline of stumps.

78
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:35,000
Elevator cables, crane booms, aircraft wings:
every engineering assumption on Earth
quietly gets rewritten.

79
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:41,000
And over generations — many, many
generations — people change too.

80
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:47,000
Shorter, stockier, denser bones,
stronger hearts.

81
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:54,000
The shape every science-fiction story gave
to high-gravity worlds, except this time
it's just us, adapting.

82
00:06:54,200 --> 00:07:00,000
A year later: the sky looks lower,
the people broader, the cities squatter,

83
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:04,000
the old bridges gone.

84
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:07,000
Catastrophe, then adaptation.

85
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:12,000
Doubling gravity doesn't end the world.

86
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:17,000
It makes everything heavier — including living.

87
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:20,000
But here's the creepiest part.

88
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:23,000
It's not the weight.

89
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:27,500
It's the forgetting.

90
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:33,000
Tonight, at midnight, you'd fight it.
You'd curse it.

91
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:41,000
And then, one ordinary morning about
a month in, you'd wake up, get out of
bed without thinking,

92
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:47,000
and carry three hundred and sixty pounds
like it was nothing —

93
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:53,000
because the scariest thing about a stronger
gravity isn't what it does to the world.

94
00:07:53,200 --> 00:08:00,000
It's what the world quietly does to survive it.

95
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:58,000
[music resolves; end card "WHAT IF?" fades in and holds; fade to black]

